WEBVTT

welcome.loop-1
00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.786
Welcome to Veradic.
Here's the whole idea in one breath.

welcome.loop-2
00:00:04.786 --> 00:00:08.752
You assign homework, and students
photograph their handwritten work.

welcome.loop-3
00:00:08.752 --> 00:00:12.543
The AI grades it as a first pass.
Then you review it and publish.

welcome.loop-4
00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:15.342
No grade reaches a student until you
publish it.

welcome.queue-5
00:00:15.744 --> 00:00:17.685
This is My Courses, your home base.

welcome.queue-6
00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:22.563
Needs you today gathers every submission that's
waiting on you, across all your classes.

welcome.queue-7
00:00:22.563 --> 00:00:26.999
Flagged work comes first, then work that's
past due and still needs your review.

welcome.queue-8
00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:29.604
Click any row to jump straight to that
student.

welcome.courses-9
00:00:30.006 --> 00:00:33.753
Further down are your courses, each
showing how much is left to review.

welcome.sidebar-10
00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:38.440
On the left, Preview as student lets you
see the app the way your students do.

welcome.sidebar-11
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.440
Video walkthrough brings back this video, and
Report a problem sends a note straight to us.

courses.concept-12
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.536
A course is a class you teach, like
Algebra I.

courses.concept-13
00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:52.809
Sections are the periods you teach it in,
and each one has its own roster.

courses.concept-14
00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:57.313
You build homework once for the course,
then assign it to any of its sections.

courses.new-15
00:00:57.715 --> 00:00:58.612
Let's make one.

courses.new-16
00:00:58.612 --> 00:01:03.755
Click New Course, give it a name, pick the
subject, and add a grade level if you like.

courses.create-17
00:01:04.157 --> 00:01:07.663
Create it, and it shows up in your list.
Open it to set it up.

courses.section-18
00:01:08.757 --> 00:01:11.344
Now add a section for each period you
teach.

courses.section-19
00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:12.990
I'll call this one Period 2.

courses.code-20
00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:16.528
Every section gets its own join code the
moment you create it.

students.code-21
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.734
The quickest way to add students is that
join code.

students.code-22
00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:24.399
Click it to copy it, then put it up on the
board.

students.code-23
00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:28.370
Students enter the code when they sign up,
and they land in this section.

students.invite-24
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.479
You can also invite students by email.
Open Manage, paste in your list, and send.

students.invite-25
00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:38.246
Each student gets an email with a link that
brings them straight into the section.

students.pending-26
00:01:38.648 --> 00:01:42.325
Each invite waits under Pending invites
until the student accepts.

students.pending-27
00:01:42.325 --> 00:01:45.945
You can resend one, or revoke it if it
went to the wrong address.

students.close-28
00:01:46.347 --> 00:01:51.480
Once everyone's in, close enrollment, so
the code stops letting new students join.

students.close-29
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.170
You can reopen it any time.

students.regen-30
00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:57.172
And if a code ever ends up somewhere it
shouldn't, regenerate it.

students.regen-31
00:01:57.172 --> 00:01:59.278
The old code stops working right away.

materials.units-32
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.126
Materials live in units.

materials.units-33
00:02:03.126 --> 00:02:05.820
Think of a unit as a chapter of your
textbook.

materials.units-34
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.033
Every homework belongs to at least one unit, so
it pays to set them up the way you teach.

materials.new_unit-35
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:13.963
Click New Unit, and name it after the
chapter.

materials.new_unit-36
00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:17.644
It appears in the list on the left, and
clicking it opens the unit.

materials.upload-37
00:02:18.046 --> 00:02:20.266
Then upload what you teach from:

materials.upload-38
00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:23.249
textbook pages, worksheets, your own
notes.

materials.upload-39
00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:25.053
Photos and PDFs both work.

materials.why-40
00:02:25.455 --> 00:02:29.619
When you have the AI write problems, you
can point it at these files, so new

materials.why-41
00:02:29.619 --> 00:02:32.577
problems match the way your class learns
the material.

materials.view-42
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:38.173
Hover over any file, and View opens it, so you
can check you uploaded the right pages.

homework.list-43
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.773
The Homework tab sorts everything by what
it needs from you:

homework.list-44
00:02:43.773 --> 00:02:46.998
homework to grade, what's due this week,
and what's finished.

homework.filter-45
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.296
As the list grows, search it, or filter by
status, section, or unit.

homework.details-46
00:02:53.008 --> 00:02:55.817
Click New homework.
Step one is the details:

homework.details-47
00:02:55.817 --> 00:02:58.116
a title, and the unit it belongs to.

homework.due-48
00:02:58.518 --> 00:03:01.056
Set a due date, and read the note
underneath.

homework.due-49
00:03:01.056 --> 00:03:03.538
The AI starts grading when that date
passes.

homework.policy-50
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.203
Pick a late policy, and choose sections.

homework.policy-51
00:03:06.203 --> 00:03:09.880
Leave sections empty to assign it to every
section in the course.

homework.generate-52
00:03:10.282 --> 00:03:11.722
Step two is the problems.

homework.generate-53
00:03:11.722 --> 00:03:15.178
The AI can write new ones from your unit
and your materials.

homework.generate-54
00:03:15.178 --> 00:03:17.598
You choose how many, and what to focus on.

homework.generate-55
00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:20.305
They land in a review queue for you to
approve.

homework.upload-56
00:03:20.707 --> 00:03:22.240
Already have a worksheet?

homework.upload-57
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.879
Switch to Upload, add photos or a PDF, and the
AI copies the problems from it word for word.

homework.grading-58
00:03:28.281 --> 00:03:29.559
Step three is grading.

homework.grading-59
00:03:29.559 --> 00:03:34.033
Set the points, then add rules of your
own, and common mistakes to watch for.

homework.grading-60
00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:38.681
On the right, you can read exactly how the
AI will give full and partial credit.

homework.review-61
00:03:39.082 --> 00:03:40.647
Step four is one last look.

homework.review-62
00:03:40.647 --> 00:03:45.227
Create and generate would start writing
problems, so for this walkthrough, I'll

homework.review-63
00:03:45.227 --> 00:03:46.793
save it as a draft instead.

homework.editor-64
00:03:47.195 --> 00:03:49.271
Saving opens the draft in the editor.

homework.editor-65
00:03:49.271 --> 00:03:52.805
Here's one that already has its problems,
each with its points.

homework.editor-66
00:03:52.805 --> 00:03:56.115
Open Solution to check the answer key and
the worked steps.

homework.publish-67
00:03:56.516 --> 00:03:59.948
When it looks right, Publish sends it out
to the sections you chose.

homework.preview-68
00:04:00.348 --> 00:04:04.932
Before you do, Preview as student shows
you exactly what your students will see,

homework.preview-69
00:04:04.932 --> 00:04:06.594
right down to the upload box.

homework.preview-70
00:04:06.594 --> 00:04:11.064
Anything you turn in here is a test run,
and your students' work is untouched.

homework.preview-71
00:04:11.064 --> 00:04:13.184
Back to teacher view brings you home.

grading.roster-72
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.923
Once homework is due, the AI grades each
submission, and you review it.

grading.roster-73
00:04:19.923 --> 00:04:24.749
This is the grading page for one section.
The roster puts what needs you on top.

grading.roster-74
00:04:24.749 --> 00:04:28.670
Needs your eyes is anything flagged, or
where the AI wasn't sure.

grading.roster-75
00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.178
Below that, AI confident.

grading.open-76
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.978
Let's open Zoe.
Her photographed work is on the right.

grading.open-77
00:04:33.978 --> 00:04:38.697
Each problem shows her answer, the answer
key, and the AI's grade, with how

grading.open-78
00:04:38.697 --> 00:04:39.704
confident it is.

grading.confident-79
00:04:40.106 --> 00:04:44.210
Problems the AI is confident about
collapse to a quick confirm, and one

grading.confident-80
00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.655
button confirms them all.

grading.confident-81
00:04:45.655 --> 00:04:48.198
Even those aren't accepted until you say
so.

grading.confident-82
00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:50.452
The one it was unsure about stays open.

grading.reason-83
00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:54.626
The AI wasn't sure about problem five, so
it left it open.

grading.reason-84
00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:55.537
Its reasoning:

grading.reason-85
00:04:55.537 --> 00:05:00.480
her work finds x equals 2 and y equals 5,
but she wrote the point backwards.

grading.reason-86
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:01.975
It suggested no credit.

grading.partial-87
00:05:02.378 --> 00:05:05.936
That's a judgment call, and it's yours.
I'll give partial credit.

grading.partial-88
00:05:05.936 --> 00:05:10.864
The feedback underneath is what Zoe sees once
the grade is published, and you can edit it.

grading.approve-89
00:05:11.264 --> 00:05:12.182
Then approve it.

grading.approve-90
00:05:12.182 --> 00:05:16.775
The AI's grade is only ever a suggestion,
and nothing is accepted automatically.

grading.publish-91
00:05:17.177 --> 00:05:19.070
To release grades, click Publish.

grading.publish-92
00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:23.714
Veradic tells you how many you haven't
approved yet, and warns you about anything

grading.publish-93
00:05:23.714 --> 00:05:26.696
still flagged.
I'll publish only the one I approved.

grading.done-94
00:05:27.098 --> 00:05:29.421
Zoe can now see her score and her
feedback.

grading.done-95
00:05:29.421 --> 00:05:31.527
Everyone else waits until you're ready.

uc.why-96
00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.367
After a student turns in work, Veradic
asks them to explain it, in their own

uc.why-97
00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:41.615
words, in a short conversation.
It's there for learning.

uc.why-98
00:05:41.615 --> 00:05:44.863
When a student is stuck, it helps them
think it through.

uc.never-99
00:05:45.265 --> 00:05:46.615
It never accuses anyone.

uc.never-100
00:05:46.615 --> 00:05:51.230
When an explanation doesn't line up with
the work, it flags it for you to look at,

uc.never-101
00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:52.862
and you decide what it means.

uc.card-102
00:05:53.264 --> 00:05:58.089
Here, Ava's answers were all correct, but she
couldn't explain how she found the slope.

uc.conversation-103
00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:03.324
Open the conversation to read exactly what
was said, and how long she took to reply.

uc.resolve-104
00:06:04.426 --> 00:06:08.271
Maybe she had an off day.
Maybe it's worth a word after class.

uc.resolve-105
00:06:08.271 --> 00:06:11.372
Either way, mark it reviewed, and pick an
outcome.

uc.resolve-106
00:06:11.372 --> 00:06:12.612
I'll choose Cleared.

uc.after-107
00:06:13.014 --> 00:06:17.014
Once it's resolved, it leaves your queue,
and the record shows you reviewed it.

grades.grid-108
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.343
The Grades tab is your gradebook:

grades.grid-109
00:06:21.343 --> 00:06:24.571
every student and every homework, one
section at a time.

grades.grid-110
00:06:24.571 --> 00:06:29.414
Only published grades show a score, and missing
work is never averaged in as a zero.

grades.bands-111
00:06:29.817 --> 00:06:32.953
The bar along the top shows how the
section is spread:

grades.bands-112
00:06:32.953 --> 00:06:37.832
three students at eighty-five or above, two in
the seventies, and two under seventy.

grades.bands-113
00:06:37.832 --> 00:06:42.246
A small dot marks a grade where you
changed the AI's suggestion, like Zoe's.

grades.attention-114
00:06:42.649 --> 00:06:46.397
Needs attention narrows it to students
averaging below seventy percent.

grades.missing-115
00:06:46.799 --> 00:06:49.927
Missing work shows who hasn't turned in
something that's past due.

grades.student-116
00:06:50.329 --> 00:06:52.043
Click a name to see one student:

grades.student-117
00:06:52.043 --> 00:06:56.222
their overall grade, what's missing, and
how they're getting on with practice.

grades.export-118
00:06:57.095 --> 00:07:01.956
Back on the gradebook, Export CSV
downloads this section's grades, ready to

grades.export-119
00:07:01.956 --> 00:07:04.872
import into Canvas, Schoology or
PowerSchool.

pi.practice-120
00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.806
Practice sets are ungraded.

pi.practice-121
00:07:08.806 --> 00:07:11.723
Students use them to work at a skill until
it clicks.

pi.practice-122
00:07:11.723 --> 00:07:14.585
The quickest way to make one is to clone a
homework.

pi.clone-123
00:07:14.988 --> 00:07:19.357
Pick the homework and the problems to
practice, and the AI writes new versions

pi.clone-124
00:07:19.357 --> 00:07:20.870
of each for you to approve.

pi.clone-125
00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.391
I won't generate one now, so I'll close
this.

pi.insights-126
00:07:23.793 --> 00:07:28.135
Student Insights shows which problems most
of a section got wrong on each homework.

pi.insights-127
00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:30.803
It only counts grades you've approved or
published.

pi.sections-128
00:07:31.205 --> 00:07:35.180
Each section gets its own view, since
different periods often trip over

pi.sections-129
00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.132
different things.

pi.sections-130
00:07:36.132 --> 00:07:38.763
Pick a section up top, and a homework
below it.

pi.reteach-131
00:07:39.165 --> 00:07:42.275
Here, every student in Period 3 missed
problem six.

pi.reteach-132
00:07:42.275 --> 00:07:46.848
It's already picked for a practice set, so
reteaching it is one click away.

pi.other-133
00:07:47.249 --> 00:07:51.889
The rest of the homework is tucked under Other
problems, if you want to add more to the set.

pi.loop-134
00:07:52.292 --> 00:07:53.267
That's the loop.

pi.loop-135
00:07:53.267 --> 00:07:58.204
Assign, let the AI take the first pass,
review and publish, then reteach what the

pi.loop-136
00:07:58.204 --> 00:08:00.277
class missed.
Thanks for watching.
